\begin{abstract}
This paper studies admissions with one open category and several reserve categories when students may qualify for more than one reserve category at once. Under the one-to-one counting rule an admitted student is counted toward at most one category, so a mechanism must decide not only who is admitted but also which category each overlapping student fills. We additionally impose a score constraint bounding the gap between the open cutoff and each reserve cutoff. Non-wastefulness has to be restated relative to that constraint; within-category fairness does not, because an upward within-category replacement can never destroy score-feasibility. We study a student-proposing score-elevated mechanism (SPSE) and show it is feasible, non-wasteful, strategy-proof and free of justified envy, but not score-feasible; since score-elevated policies are known to breach cutoff rules already without overlap, we isolate what overlap adds, namely the routing of a multiply eligible student as an independent cause. We then show that score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness are jointly unattainable \emph{even when every reserve category is individually fillable score-feasibly}; the obstruction is a failure of Hall's condition on the induced feasibility graph and vanishes without overlap. Finally we give a preference-aware overlapping de-reservation mechanism (PA-ODR) that treats score-feasibility as hard and reallocates unfulfillable reserve capacity. PA-ODR restores score-feasibility and retains within-category fairness unweakened, but forfeits strategy-proofness and freedom from justified envy, and de-reserves an order-dependent, non-minimal amount.

\keywords{Matching \and Affirmative action \and Reserve categories \and Overlapping types \and De-reservation \and School choice}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Reserve-based admissions and controlled school choice have been studied extensively \cite{Abdu05a,EHYY14a,HYY13a,EcYe15a,AyTu20a,AyTu23a}. In the most standard formulations each student belongs to a single protected category or to none. In many real systems, however, protected categories overlap. S\"onmez and Yenmez study overlapping reserves \cite{SoYe20a} and the vertical--horizontal reservation system of Indian affirmative action \cite{SoYe22a}; related overlapping-type environments are studied by Kurata et al.\ \cite{KHIY17a}, Ayg\"un and B\'o \cite{AyBo21a} and Delacr\'etaz et al.\ \cite{DKT23a}, and more broadly by Goto et al.\ \cite{GKK+17a} and Kamada and Kojima \cite{KaKo24a} under distributional constraints. Indian engineering admissions, the setting closest to ours, are described by Baswana et al.\ \cite{BCC+19b}. That \emph{which} slot a student is counted against is itself a design variable is made explicit by Kominers and S\"onmez \cite{KoSo16a}, and the cost of getting the accounting wrong is documented by Dur et al.\ \cite{DKPS18a}.

Once categories overlap, a mechanism must decide not only which students are admitted but how overlapping students are counted, since under one-to-one counting an overlapping student fills at most one reserve slot. The category assigned to a student is therefore not a bookkeeping detail but part of the allocation. We add a second ingredient, a score constraint keeping the open cutoff and each reserve cutoff close. This is not a stylized device: in \emph{Ashoka Kumar Thakur vs.\ Union of India} the Supreme Court of India recommended a maximum ten-point difference between the open and OBC cutoffs, and Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b} analyze that directive; cutoff-based concerns appear more generally in \cite{AyTu23a,ABB24a}. Under such a rule a reserve seat may be nominally available yet unusable, because every remaining eligible student would violate the bound.

These ingredients affect the standard desiderata \cite{EHYY14a,KHIY17a,GKK+17a} asymmetrically. Non-wastefulness must be restated: a seat is not available unless it can be filled without breaching the score rule. Within-category fairness need not be, because replacing a matched student by a higher-scoring eligible one can only raise that category's cutoff (Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf}), so the fairness notion below is not an artificially weakened one.

\subsubsection{Relation to the closest prior work.}
S\"onmez and Yenmez \cite{SoYe20a,SoYe22a} take reserve capacities as given and impose no cutoff-gap requirement, which is the constraint driving every negative result here. Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b} study exactly the score-elevated policy and the ten-point rule, but without overlapping eligibility, so their mechanism never decides \emph{which} reserve category a student fills. We should be explicit about what is new. That a score-elevated mechanism can breach a cutoff-gap rule is \emph{not} new and needs no overlap; it is the inconsistency of \cite{AyTu23b}. What overlap adds is a second, independent cause: when a student is eligible for several categories but counted once, the routing decision itself determines whether the cutoff rule survives, and a routing optimal for the student can destroy a score-feasibility that another routing preserves. Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} exhibits this cause and Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause} separates it from the first; Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} makes the same separation for the existence question by hypothesis. The de-reservation response is in the spirit of \cite{AyTu23a}.

\subsubsection{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item We formalize an overlapping-reserve model with a common score bonus and a cutoff-gap constraint, restate non-wastefulness relative to it, and show within-category fairness needs no such restatement (Section~\ref{sec:model}).
\item We study a student-proposing score-elevated mechanism (SPSE), following the deferred-acceptance tradition of Gale and Shapley \cite{GaSh62a} and the design literature synthesized in \cite{AbSo03b,Roth08a}. It is feasible, non-wasteful, envy-free and strategy-proof but may breach score-feasibility, and we separate the two causes of that breach, only the second being new (Section~\ref{sec:spse}).
\item We show score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness are jointly unattainable \emph{even when every reserve category is individually fillable score-feasibly}, so the obstruction is a Hall-condition failure rather than a shortage of strong candidates, and it disappears without overlap (Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility}).
\item We propose a preference-aware overlapping de-reservation mechanism (PA-ODR), proving termination, polynomial running time, feasibility, score-feasibility and non-wastefulness relative to the final capacities, and \emph{unweakened} within-category fairness. We then delimit its price: justified envy, manipulability, order-dependent non-minimal de-reservation, and de-reservation of seats that could have been left idle (Section~\ref{sec:paodr}).
\item We realize the reserve-side selection rule as one maximum-weight bipartite matching, and observe that minimum de-reservation is decidable by enumeration, polynomially for bounded $|T|$; the unbounded case is open (Remark~\ref{rem:computation}, Proposition~\ref{prop:minderes}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Model}
\label{sec:model}

\subsection{Primitives, Counting and Matchings}

We consider admissions to a single school. Let $I$ be a finite set of students, $T$ a finite set of reserve categories, $o$ the open category, and $V=T\cup\{o\}$. Each $c\in V$ has capacity $q^c\in\mathbb{N}$; each $i\in I$ has a raw score $\sigma(i)\in\R_+$, assumed distinct across students, and a reserve-eligibility set $\T(i)\subseteq T$. Student $i$ has \emph{overlapping} categories when $|\T(i)|\ge2$. All reserve categories share a common bonus $k\ge0$, and $\delta\ge0$ is the allowed gap between the open and reserve cutoffs; the bonus does not vary by category. The admissible category set of $i$ is $V_i:=\{o\}\cup\T(i)$, and $i$ has a strict preference $\succ_i$ over $V_i$, with $r_i(c)$ the rank of $c$ ($1$ = best). Since there is a single school, students rank admission routes rather than schools, and a student cannot apply where she lacks eligibility.

We adopt \emph{one-to-one counting}: a matched student is counted toward exactly one category chosen by the mechanism, rather than toward every category for which she is eligible. This is the convention of the literature on overlapping types, multidimensional affirmative action and diversity constraints \cite{KHIY17a,AyBo21a,GKK+17a}, and it models systems in which a student qualifying under, say, both a socioeconomic and a geographic criterion may enter through either route but cannot fill both quotas. An allocation is therefore a matching between students and categories.

\begin{definition}[Matching and feasibility]
A matching is a tuple $\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}$ with $\mu_c\subseteq I$; write $M(\mu):=\bigcup_{c\in V}\mu_c$ for the matched students. It is \emph{feasible} if (i) $|\mu_c|\le q^c$ for every $c\in V$, (ii) $\mu_c\cap\mu_{c'}=\emptyset$ for distinct $c,c'$, and (iii) $c\in V_i$ for every $i\in\mu_c$.
\end{definition}

Condition (ii) is the formal statement of one-to-one counting.

\subsection{Adjusted Scores, Cutoffs and Score-Feasibility}

The open category ranks by raw score, reserve categories by score plus the common bonus. Since $i$ can only be assigned within $V_i$, it suffices to define, for $c\in V_i$, the \emph{adjusted score} $\sigma_c(i):=\sigma(i)$ if $c=o$ and $\sigma_c(i):=\sigma(i)+k$ if $c=t\in\T(i)$. Reserve categories thus share one score ranking over their eligible students; differences across them come from eligibility, capacities and preferences. The case $k=0$ recovers the baseline in which every category ranks by raw score, and is covered throughout.

\begin{definition}[Cutoffs and score-feasibility]
\label{def:scorefeas}
For a matching $\mu$ let $e_o(\mu):=\min_{i\in\mu_o}\sigma(i)$, with $e_o(\mu)=-\infty$ if $\mu_o=\emptyset$, and for $t\in T$ let $e_t^+(\mu):=\min_{i\in\mu_t}\sigma_t(i)$, with $e_t^+(\mu)=+\infty$ if $\mu_t=\emptyset$. A feasible $\mu$ is \emph{score-feasible} if $e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le\delta$ for every $t\in T$.
\end{definition}

We read $e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)$ as $-\infty$ whenever $\mu_o=\emptyset$ or $\mu_t=\emptyset$, including when both are (where the expression would be indeterminate), so the condition is vacuous there and needs no side condition. The point is that no reserve cutoff may fall too far below the open one, measured on the adjusted scale: the bonus is treated as institutionally legitimate, and $\delta$ bounds only the gap remaining after that sanctioned adjustment. The two parameters therefore do different work and do not grant the advantage twice, even though in raw scores they compound, a reserve admit falling up to $\delta+k$ below the open cutoff. When $k=0$ they coincide and $\delta$ alone bounds the raw gap, which is the ten-point rule of \cite{AyTu23b}. Note finally that score-feasibility is an ex post requirement on realized cutoffs, not something implied by the within-category ranking rule; this is why a second mechanism is needed in Section~\ref{sec:paodr}.

\subsection{Desiderata}

Wastefulness is usually defined by an empty seat an unmatched student could occupy. Here that is not enough: she can occupy it only while preserving score-feasibility.

\begin{definition}[Non-wastefulness]
\label{def:nw}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is \emph{non-wasteful} if there are no unmatched $i\in I\setminus M(\mu)$ and $c\in V_i$ such that adding $i$ to $c$ yields a matching that is both feasible and score-feasible.
\end{definition}

So a reserve seat may be empty yet unusable. Two readings matter. Non-wastefulness is stated for feasible, not only score-feasible, matchings, because SPSE below is non-wasteful without being score-feasible; and the requirement is that the \emph{enlarged} matching be score-feasible, not that score-feasibility be preserved, since the latter would declare every score-infeasible matching vacuously non-wasteful. The same reading is used in Definition~\ref{def:nw-hat}.

\begin{definition}[Within-category fairness]
\label{def:fair}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is \emph{fair within categories} if there are no $t\in T$ and students $i,j$ with (i) $t\in\T(i)\cap\T(j)$, (ii) $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$, (iii) $j\in\mu_t$, and (iv) $i\notin M(\mu)$.
\end{definition}

Under one-to-one counting the relevant condition is that $j$ is assigned to $t$, not merely eligible for it. The definition ranges over reserve categories only, deliberately: it is the minimal notion on which our two mechanisms agree, and it isolates the guarantee that a reserve seat is never held by someone a stronger eligible claimant could displace. Complaints aimed at the open category, and complaints by already-matched students, belong to Definition~\ref{def:nje}, which is where the mechanisms part company.

Definition~\ref{def:fair} makes no reference to the score constraint, and one might expect it to, by analogy with Definition~\ref{def:nw}. The following shows such a weakening would have no content.

\begin{lemma}[Within-category fairness needs no score-relative weakening]
\label{lem:fair-sf}
Let $\mu$ be feasible, $t\in T$, $j\in\mu_t$, and $i\notin M(\mu)$ with $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$. Let $\mu'$ replace $j$ by $i$ in $\mu_t$. Then $\mu'$ is feasible, and if $\mu$ is score-feasible so is $\mu'$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Feasibility is immediate: $|\mu'_t|=|\mu_t|\le q^t$, $i$ was assigned nowhere so nobody is counted twice, and $t\in\T(i)\subseteq V_i$. The swap changes no other category and leaves $\mu_o$ untouched, so $e_o(\mu')=e_o(\mu)$ and $e_{t'}^+(\mu')=e_{t'}^+(\mu)$ for $t'\neq t$. For $t$, $\mu'_t=(\mu_t\setminus\{j\})\cup\{i\}$ with $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$ gives $e_t^+(\mu')\ge e_t^+(\mu)$, hence $e_o(\mu')-e_t^+(\mu')\le e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le\delta$.
\end{proof}

An upward within-category replacement therefore never endangers the cutoff rule. The asymmetry with non-wastefulness is genuine: adding a \emph{new} student to a reserve category can lower its cutoff, whereas \emph{replacing} one by a better student cannot. This is why only Definition~\ref{def:nw} carries a score-feasibility clause.

The next requirement is the standard no-justified-envy condition transposed to categories. It also lets a \emph{matched} student complain about a category she ranks higher, and admits the open category as an object of complaint.

\begin{definition}[No justified envy]
\label{def:nje}
A feasible matching $\mu$ has \emph{no justified envy} if there are no student $i$ and category $c\in V_i$ with (i) either $i\notin M(\mu)$, or $i\in\mu_{c'}$ for some $c'\in V_i$ with $c\succ_i c'$; and (ii) either $|\mu_c|<q^c$, or some $j\in\mu_c$ has $\sigma_c(j)<\sigma_c(i)$.
\end{definition}

No justified envy implies within-category fairness: if $i\notin M(\mu)$, $j\in\mu_t$ and $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$ with $t\in\T(i)$, then $c=t$ witnesses justified envy. The converse fails by Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy}.

Unlike Definition~\ref{def:fair}, leaving Definition~\ref{def:nje} without a score-feasibility clause is a genuine choice, because Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf} does not extend to it: clause (ii) also lets $i$ claim a \emph{vacant} seat, which can lower that category's cutoff, and clause (i) lets a matched student leave $c'$, which for $c'=o$ \emph{raises} the open cutoff and can break score-feasibility elsewhere. A score-relative weakening would thus have content. We use the unweakened notion because it strengthens Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-nje}; the price is that the negative Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy} must be checked against the weaker reading, which we do there.

Finally, a structural circularity underlies everything: feasible reserve assignments depend on the open cutoff, while the open cutoff depends on who is left after reserve assignment. Score-feasibility is thus not a static eligibility condition, and greedy quota-filling is unreliable. SPSE responds by keeping capacities fixed and using deferred-acceptance logic; PA-ODR treats the cutoff rule as hard and de-reserves, following the policy logic of \cite{AyTu23a}.

\section{Student-Proposing Score-Elevated Mechanism}
\label{sec:spse}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Student-Proposing Score-Elevated Mechanism (SPSE)}
\label{alg:spse}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $I$, $V=T\cup\{o\}$, capacities $(q^c)_{c\in V}$, preferences $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$, scores $\sigma$, eligibility $\T(\cdot)$, bonus $k$
\Ensure Matching $\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}$
\State $\mu_c\gets\emptyset$ for all $c\in V$; mark every student unmatched
\State Set $V_i:=\{o\}\cup\T(i)$ and compute $\sigma_c(i)$ for every $i$ and every $c\in V_i$
\While{some unmatched $i$ still has an unapplied category in $V_i$}
    \State $i$ applies to her most preferred unapplied $c\in V_i$
    \State $c$ considers $\mu_c\cup\{i\}$ and keeps at most $q^c$ students with highest $\sigma_c$
    \State Every rejected student becomes unmatched
\EndWhile
\State \Return $\mu$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

SPSE preserves one-to-one counting because a student is held by at most one category at a time. It differs from ordinary student-proposing deferred acceptance only in that the objects proposed to are categories and in the reserve-side score elevation. Each student applies to each category of $V_i$ at most once, so the loop runs at most $|I|(|T|+1)$ times and the algorithm terminates.

\begin{proposition}[Feasibility and non-wastefulness]
\label{prop:spse-basic}
The output of SPSE is feasible and non-wasteful in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:nw}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Each category keeps at most its capacity at every step, no student is held twice, and $i$ applies only within $V_i$, giving feasibility. For non-wastefulness, suppose unmatched $i$ could be added to $c\in V_i$ with the result feasible and score-feasible; feasibility of the enlargement needs $|\mu_c|<q^c$. As $i$ is unmatched at termination, the loop guard forces her to have applied to every category of $V_i$, including $c$, and to have been rejected by $c$. But a category rejects only when full, and once full never releases capacity, only swapping a held student for a strictly better applicant. Hence $|\mu_c|=q^c$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[No justified envy]
\label{prop:spse-nje}
The output of SPSE has no justified envy, and is therefore fair within categories.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mu$ be the output and let $i,c\in V_i$ satisfy clause (i) of Definition~\ref{def:nje}. In either case $c$ is strictly better for $i$ than her final assignment; since she applies in $\succ_i$-order and is held by a category only until it rejects her, she can end unmatched or strictly worse off only after $c$ rejected her. At that rejection $c$ held $q^c$ students and rejected the lowest-$\sigma_c$ member of $\mu_c\cup\{i\}$, so, scores being distinct, every retained student has adjusted score strictly above $\sigma_c(i)$. Thereafter $c$ never drops below $q^c$ and only replaces held students by strictly better ones, so at termination $|\mu_c|=q^c$ and $\sigma_c(j)>\sigma_c(i)$ for all $j\in\mu_c$. Both alternatives of clause (ii) fail. The second statement follows from the implication after Definition~\ref{def:nje}.
\end{proof}

A student's raw score and eligibility are verifiable, so the only object she can misreport is $\succ_i$.

\begin{proposition}[Strategy-proofness]
\label{prop:spse-sp}
No student can obtain a strictly $\succ_i$-better assignment under SPSE by reporting an ordering over $V_i$ other than $\succ_i$, holding others' reports fixed.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix $\sigma$, $\T(\cdot)$, $k$ and $(q^c)_{c\in V}$. Treat each $c\in V$ as a college of capacity $q^c$ whose priority over its acceptable students---those $i$ with $c\in V_i$---is the strict ranking induced by $\sigma_c$, well defined as raw scores are distinct. These priorities are responsive to $q^c$, since $c$ always retains the $q^c$ highest-$\sigma_c$ applicants available. Each $i$ strictly ranks $V_i$, all of it acceptable. The economy is thus an instance of the college-admissions model of Gale and Shapley \cite{GaSh62a} and Algorithm~\ref{alg:spse} is student-proposing deferred acceptance on it; strategy-proofness for the proposing side is the theorem of Dubins and Freedman \cite{DuFr81a} and Roth \cite{Roth82a}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[SPSE does not guarantee score-feasibility]
\label{prop:spse-not-sf}
The output of SPSE need not be score-feasible, even when a matching that is simultaneously score-feasible, non-wasteful and fair within categories exists.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, and take $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(82,\{t_2\})$ with $t_1\succ_{i_2}t_2\succ_{i_2}o$ and $t_2\succ_{i_3}o$; so $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100$ and $\sigma_{t_2}(i_3)=87$. In round~1, $i_1$ applies to $o$, $i_2$ to $t_1$ and $i_3$ to $t_2$; each category receives one applicant and holds her, so SPSE terminates with $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, $\mu_{t_2}=\{i_3\}$. Then $e_o(\mu)-e_{t_2}^+(\mu)=100-87=13>10=\delta$, violating score-feasibility.

Consider instead $\nu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\nu_{t_2}=\{i_2\}$, $\nu_{t_1}=\emptyset$. Here $e_o(\nu)=e_{t_2}^+(\nu)=100$, so the gap is $0$, and the constraint for the empty $t_1$ is vacuous; $\nu$ is score-feasible. It is non-wasteful, since the only unmatched student $i_3$ has $V_{i_3}=\{t_2,o\}$ and both are at capacity; and fair within categories, since the sole member of $t_2$ satisfies $\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100>87=\sigma_{t_2}(i_3)$.
\end{proof}

We do not claim $\nu$ dominates: SPSE matches all three students while $\nu$ matches two, and $\nu$ itself has justified envy, as $i_2$ prefers $t_1$ and $|\nu_{t_1}|=0<q^{t_1}$. What the proposition establishes is that the three listed properties do not force score-feasibility, so score-feasibility is an independent requirement, bought here at the price of a seat---the same trade-off Section~\ref{sec:paodr} pays in reserve capacity. It should also not be read as showing that score-elevated mechanisms breach cutoff rules \emph{only} under overlap.

\begin{nremark}[Two causes of score-infeasibility, one of them new]
\label{rem:overlap-cause}
Score-elevated policies can breach a cutoff-gap rule with no overlap at all; this is the inconsistency of Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b}. Take $T=\{t_1\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, and $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(80,\{t_1\})$ with $t_1\succ_{i_2}o$. SPSE returns $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$ and $e_o-e_{t_1}^+=100-85=15>\delta$. Here the sole eligible candidate is simply too weak and no assignment decision is at fault; the matching leaving $t_1$ empty is score-feasible, non-wasteful and fair, so even the bare statement of Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} already holds without overlap.

Overlap adds a second, logically independent cause. In the instance above both $t_1$ and $t_2$ are acceptable homes for $i_2$ and her adjusted score is the same in either, so no candidate is too weak: $i_2$ alone would satisfy the cutoff rule in whichever category she filled. SPSE resolves the choice by $i_2$'s own preference, sending her to $t_1$ and leaving $t_2$ to the far weaker $i_3$. Under one-to-one counting it is that routing, not the applicant pool, that breaks the rule, and the same pool admits a score-feasible routing. This cause cannot arise under single eligibility, since then there is nothing to route. The rest of the paper concerns it, and Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} is stated so as to exclude the first cause.
\end{nremark}

\subsection{An Impossibility Driven by Overlap}

An impossibility here is easy to obtain for the uninteresting reason just described: in the two-student instance of Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause}, open-completeness forces $e_o=100$, nobody clears the bar, and reserve-completeness fails on its own. We therefore build the negation of that reason into the hypothesis.

\begin{definition}[Individual completability]
\label{def:indcomp}
An instance is \emph{individually completable} if for every $t\in T$ there is a feasible matching that is score-feasible, satisfies open-completeness, and has $|\mu_t|=q^t$.
\end{definition}

So every announced quota can be honoured in full, score-feasibly and without disturbing the open category, provided one may ignore the other reserve categories meanwhile. The impossibility is that these separately keepable promises cannot be kept together.

\begin{proposition}[Impossibility under full capacity utilization]
\label{prop:impossibility}
There is an individually completable instance (Definition~\ref{def:indcomp}) admitting no feasible matching that simultaneously satisfies score-feasibility, \emph{open-completeness} and \emph{reserve-completeness}, where open-completeness requires $\mu_o$ to consist of the $q^o$ highest raw-score students in $I$, and reserve-completeness requires $|\mu_t|=q^t$ for every $t$ for which at least $q^t$ students are eligible.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, and take $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(80,\{t_1\})$, $i_4(78,\{t_2\})$. Open-completeness forces $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, so $e_o(\mu)=100$ and score-feasibility requires $\sigma_t(i)\ge90$ for every reserve assignee. Since $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100\ge90$ while $\sigma_{t_1}(i_3)=85<90$ and $\sigma_{t_2}(i_4)=83<90$, student $i_2$ is the only one placeable in any reserve category.

The instance is individually completable: for $t_1$ take $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, $\mu_{t_2}=\emptyset$, which is open-complete with $|\mu_{t_1}|=q^{t_1}$ and $e_o-e_{t_1}^+=0\le\delta$, the empty $t_2$ being vacuous; exchanging $t_1$ and $t_2$ handles $t_2$. Yet both quotas cannot be met at once: $t_1$ and $t_2$ have eligible sets $\{i_2,i_3\}$ and $\{i_2,i_4\}$, so reserve-completeness demands $|\mu_{t_1}|=|\mu_{t_2}|=1$, requiring two distinct reserve placements, while one-to-one counting lets $i_2$ count toward at most one. Some reserve category must then admit $i_3$ or $i_4$, breaching score-feasibility.
\end{proof}

Both completeness axioms are institutional commitments rather than normative optima. Open-completeness says the open category is genuinely open, filled by raw merit from the whole pool with nobody held back as more useful elsewhere. Reserve-completeness says an announced quota must be honoured whenever the eligible population suffices, which is how quotas are written in policy documents: eligibility is counted category by category, precisely because the policy is drafted without reference to overlap. Weakening it to demand only what is \emph{jointly} fillable would restore consistency but concede the point, namely that overlap silently reduces what a stated quota delivers. PA-ODR makes that concession explicit as de-reservation.

\begin{nremark}[The obstruction is overlap, not weak candidates]
\label{rem:hall}
Individual completability rules out the familiar shortage-of-candidates explanation, and it is worth naming what replaces it. Let $G(e)$ be the feasibility graph of Section~\ref{sec:paodr} induced by $e=100$ and let $N(S)$ be the students adjacent to some category in $S\subseteq T$. Then $N(\{t_1,t_2\})=\{i_2\}$, so $|N(\{t_1,t_2\})|=1<2=q^{t_1}+q^{t_2}$ and the capacitated Hall condition $|N(S)|\ge\sum_{t\in S}q^t$ fails. The hypotheses now sit in a clean relation: individual completability is Hall's condition restricted to singletons, and the impossibility is that this does not imply it for larger $S$. The two coincide when the $N(\{t\})$ are pairwise disjoint, so the gap between them is exactly overlap, and the impossibility vanishes if no student is eligible for two categories. This also explains why the next mechanism is built around a bipartite matching rather than category-by-category quota filling.
\end{nremark}

\section{Preference-Aware Overlapping De-Reservation}
\label{sec:paodr}

\subsection{Feasibility Graph and Preference-Aware Lexicographic Matching}

For a fixed open cutoff $e$, let $G(e)$ be the bipartite graph whose left side is the students not assigned to the open category, whose right side is $T$, and with an edge $(i,t)$ iff $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$. With a common bonus, a student eligible for several categories is score-admissible either for all of them at $e$ or for none; the graph is still needed because one-to-one counting forces a choice of which one she fills.

Write $P(e)$ for the edge set, the \emph{admissible pairs}, and fix an injective index $p:P(e)\to\{1,\dots,|P(e)|\}$ from fixed linear orders on $I$ and $T$. Among capacitated matchings on $G(e)$, \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is the one selected by, in order: (1)~maximize the number of matched pairs; (2)~lexicographically maximize the weakly decreasingly sorted vector of matched elevated scores $\sigma_t(i)=\sigma(i)+k$; (3)~lexicographically minimize the weakly decreasingly sorted vector of preference ranks $r_i(t)$; (4)~minimize $\sum_{(i,t)\in\mu}2^{p(i,t)}$.

Criterion 3 compares matchings by worst preference rank first, then second-worst, so it is egalitarian. Criterion 4 is a pure tie-breaker with no normative content, and it is needed: if two students are eligible for the same two unit-capacity categories and rank them alike, the two assignments have identical cardinality, score vectors and rank vectors. As distinct edge sets give distinct sums of distinct powers of two, criterion 4 always resolves such ties and \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is well defined. The division of labour between criteria 2 and 3 is sharper than it looks: the first two already pin down \emph{who} is admitted, leaving 3 and 4 to decide only \emph{where}.

\begin{lemma}[Criteria 1 and 2 determine the admitted set]
\label{lem:matroid}
Fix $e$ and right-side capacities $(\hat q^t)_{t\in T}$. All capacitated matchings on $G(e)$ satisfying criteria 1 and 2 match exactly the same set of students; criteria 3 and 4 only choose its assignment to categories.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Replace each $t$ by $\hat q^t$ parallel copies, turning capacitated matchings into ordinary bipartite matchings. The student sets matchable in a bipartite graph are exactly the independent sets of the induced transversal matroid $\mathcal{M}$, and criterion 1 restricts to its bases. Weight $i$ by $\sigma(i)+k$; these are pairwise distinct as raw scores are. Criterion 2 selects the bases whose decreasingly sorted weight vector is lexicographically maximal, which for a matroid with distinct weights is exactly the output of the greedy algorithm scanning students in decreasing weight. Greedy is deterministic, so the basis is unique.
\end{proof}

Criterion 2 thus fixes the admitted score profile and criterion 3 distributes overlapping students across categories, so preferences matter only after size and score have been optimized. The matroid structure here is the one underlying Fleiner's fixed-point treatment of stable matching \cite{Flei03a} and the substitutable choice functions of \cite{GKK+17a,KoSo16a}; what is specific is that the ground set is defined by an endogenous cutoff.

\begin{nremark}[Computation]
\label{rem:computation}
\textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is computable in polynomial time. As the common bonus makes $\sigma_t(i)$ independent of $t$, let $s(i)$ be the rank of $\sigma(i)+k$ increasing among distinct elevated scores incident to $P(e)$, put $n:=|P(e)|\le|I||T|$ and $B:=n+1$, and weight each admissible pair by
\[
w(i,t) := C_1 + C_2\cdot 2^{\,s(i)} - C_3\cdot B^{\,r_i(t)} - 2^{\,p(i,t)},
\]
with $C_3>\sum_{P(e)}2^{p(i,t)}$, $C_2>\sum_{P(e)}(C_3B^{r_i(t)}+2^{p(i,t)})$ and $C_1>\sum_{P(e)}|C_2 2^{s(i)}-C_3B^{r_i(t)}-2^{p(i,t)}|$. A maximum-weight capacitated bipartite matching under $w$ realizes the four criteria in order.

The $C_1$ term maximizes cardinality. One line of care is needed, since naively comparing sizes $m+1$ and $m$ would require $C_1$ to exceed \emph{twice} the variation of the rest; it does not, because the choice of $C_2$ makes every non-$C_1$ contribution positive ($s(i)\ge1$ gives $C_2 2^{s(i)}\ge2C_2>C_3B^{r_i(t)}+2^{p(i,t)}$). Writing $R(\mu)$ for that contribution, $0\le R(\mu)<C_1$, so size $m+1$ beats size $m$ by $C_1+R(\mu)-R(\mu')>0$. Among maximum-cardinality matchings the $C_2$ term dominates, and comparing $\sum2^{s(i)}$ as a binary number is exactly lexicographic comparison of the sorted score vector: no carrying occurs, since a matching uses each student once and raw scores are distinct, so its exponents $s(i)$ are distinct. This is why base $2$ is safe for scores but \emph{not} for ranks: ranks are shared across students, and $\sum2^{r_i(t)}$ would carry---the multisets $\{3,1,1\}$ and $\{2,2,2\}$ both sum to $12$ although the second is strictly better under criterion 3. Taking $B$ above the size of any matching makes the base-$B$ digits of $\sum B^{r_i(t)}$ count matched pairs of each rank, so comparing these integers is exactly lexicographic comparison of the sorted rank vectors. The $-2^p$ term implements criterion 4, its exponents being distinct by construction.

The largest weight is bounded by a product of $2^{|I|+1}$, $B^{|T|+2}$ and $2^{n+1}$, so weights need $O(|I||T|)$ bits and the computation is polynomial, e.g.\ via min-cost flow; the subproblem is a standard capacitated assignment fitting the framework of \cite{GKK+17a}. The single-weight form certifies polynomiality in one step but is a heavy instrument: by Lemma~\ref{lem:matroid} an implementation may instead compute the admitted set by matroid greedy and then solve an ordinary min-cost assignment with costs $C_3B^{r_i(t)}+2^{p(i,t)}$.
\end{nremark}

\subsection{The Algorithm}

The mechanism is stated as Algorithm~\ref{alg:paodr}.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Preference-Aware Overlapping De-Reservation (PA-ODR)}
\label{alg:paodr}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $I$, scores $\sigma$, eligibility $\T(\cdot)$, preferences $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$, capacities $q^o,(q^t)_{t\in T}$, bonus $k$, gap $\delta$, a fixed linear order $\prec$ on $T$
\Ensure Matching $\mu=(\mu_o,(\mu_t)_{t\in T})$
\State $\hat q^o\gets q^o$ and $\hat q^t\gets q^t$ for all $t\in T$
\Loop
    \State Assign the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ raw-score students to $o$; call this set $\mu_o$
    \State $e\gets\min_{i\in\mu_o}\sigma(i)$ if $\mu_o\neq\emptyset$, else $e\gets-\infty$
    \State Build $G(e)$ with edge $(i,t)$ iff $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$, giving $t$ capacity $\hat q^t$
    \State Compute $(\mu_t)_{t\in T}$ by \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}
    \If{there exists $t$ with $|\mu_t|<\hat q^t$}
        \State Let $t^\ast$ be the $\prec$-first such category
        \State $\hat q^{t^\ast}\gets\hat q^{t^\ast}-1$ and $\hat q^o\gets\hat q^o+1$
    \Else
        \State \Return $\mu=(\mu_o,(\mu_t)_{t\in T})$
    \EndIf
\EndLoop
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

PA-ODR treats score-feasibility as a hard institutional constraint. The open category is filled first by raw score; reserve assignment then runs only over pairs feasible at the induced cutoff; capacity a reserve category cannot fill is de-reserved to the open category. So the mechanism preserves merit ordering in the open category, uses elevated scores in reserve categories, and uses preferences only to break ties among already-feasible placements, but it may change the effective quotas. The order $\prec$ is part of its specification and makes the output well defined; Section~\ref{sec:order} discusses what is at stake in choosing it. Note that PA-ODR is preference-aware only on the reserve side: a student who strictly prefers a reserve category to $o$ may still be placed in $o$, and Section~\ref{sec:paodr-limits} shows this is what costs PA-ODR both envy-freeness and strategy-proofness.

\begin{nremark}[What triggers de-reservation]
\label{sec:trigger}
The stopping rule is stronger than score-feasibility alone requires: PA-ODR halts when $|\mu_t|=\hat q^t$ for every $t$, i.e.\ when the outcome is reserve-complete for the \emph{current} capacities. It therefore de-reserves whenever a reserve seat cannot be filled, not merely when the score rule would otherwise fail. The difference is real. On the instance of Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf}, the first iteration gives $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $e=100$, admissibility needs $\sigma(i)\ge85$, only $i_2$ qualifies, so one category is short and PA-ODR de-reserves, ending at $\hat q=(2,0,1)$ with $\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2\}$, $\mu_{t_2}=\{i_3\}$. Yet $\nu$ of that proposition is score-feasible, non-wasteful and fair \emph{under the original capacities}: leaving a seat idle would have sufficed. The defence of the design is coverage---PA-ODR matches all three students, $\nu$ only two---and its cost is that a quota is retired rather than merely unmet, which an idle seat is not. An idle-seat variant halting when no further edge can be added is well defined and score-feasible by the same argument, but forfeits that coverage gain; we do not pursue it. All results below, and \textsc{MinDeres} in Section~\ref{sec:order}, are relative to the reserve-completeness rule.
\end{nremark}

\begin{lemma}[The open cutoff falls monotonically]
\label{lem:monotone}
If $e^{(1)},e^{(2)},\dots$ are the open cutoffs of successive iterations, then $e^{(1)}\ge e^{(2)}\ge\cdots$ and $\mu_o^{(1)}\subseteq\mu_o^{(2)}\subseteq\cdots$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each non-terminating iteration raises $\hat q^o$ by one and leaves $\sigma$ unchanged. As $\mu_o$ is always the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ students by raw score, these sets are nested in $\hat q^o$, and $e$ is the minimum over a weakly larger set.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:monotone} exposes two opposing forces. Lowering $e$ relaxes $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$, so edges are only added to $G(e)$; but the same step moves one more student into $\mu_o$, so left vertices are only removed. Neither dominates, which is why a single de-reservation can fail to help, as the second branch of Example~\ref{ex:order} shows.

\subsection{Order Dependence and Non-Minimality}
\label{sec:order}

De-reservation enables otherwise infeasible reserve assignment but destroys reserve capacity, so a natural requirement is to de-reserve as few seats as possible. PA-ODR fails it, because each decision changes both the cutoff and the remaining pool.

\begin{example}[The order $\prec$ changes how many seats are de-reserved]
\label{ex:order}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, with $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(84,\{t_1\})$, $i_3(83,\{t_2\})$. In the first iteration $\hat q=(1,1,1)$, so $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $e=100$; admissibility needs $\sigma(i)+5\ge90$, and neither $89$ nor $88$ clears it, so $G(100)$ has no edges and both categories are unfilled.

\emph{If $t_1\prec t_2$}, the algorithm de-reserves $t_1$, giving $\hat q=(2,0,1)$, so $\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2\}$, $e=84$ and admissibility needs $\sigma(i)+5\ge74$. Then $i_3$ qualifies, the edge $(i_3,t_2)$ appears, and the algorithm returns $\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2\}$, $\mu_{t_2}=\{i_3\}$, having de-reserved one seat.

\emph{If $t_2\prec t_1$}, it de-reserves $t_2$, giving $\hat q=(2,1,0)$, and again $\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2\}$ with $e=84$; but $i_2$, the only student eligible for $t_1$, has been absorbed into the open category, so $G(84)$ has no edge into $t_1$ and $|\mu_{t_1}|=0<1$ triggers a second de-reservation. The algorithm returns $\mu_o=\{i_1,i_2,i_3\}$, having de-reserved two seats.
\end{example}

Both runs match all three students, so the difference is not coverage but institutional: under one order a reserve quota survives and $i_3$ enters through $t_2$, under the other both quotas are destroyed and $i_3$ enters through $o$. Since admitting students through the reserve route is the point of a reserve system, this is substantive, and PA-ODR does not minimize de-reservation. Unlike Section~\ref{sec:spse}, this phenomenon is not overlap-driven and we do not present it as such: no student in Example~\ref{ex:order} is eligible for two categories. It is the cutoff feedback of Lemma~\ref{lem:monotone} and would arise under single eligibility too; overlap worsens it rather than causing it, since an absorbed overlapping student can empty several categories at once.

We therefore state PA-ODR's properties relative to the capacity vector it produces. The minimization problem need not be left open. Call $\hat q$ with $\hat q^t\le q^t$ and $\hat q^o=q^o+\sum_{t}(q^t-\hat q^t)$ \emph{realizable} if, on filling $o$ with the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ raw scores and inducing $G(e)$, that graph admits a capacitated matching saturating every $\hat q^t$; \textsc{MinDeres} asks for a realizable $\hat q$ minimizing $\sum_t(q^t-\hat q^t)$. It is always solvable, as $\hat q^t\equiv0$ is vacuously realizable. Realizability, like the stopping rule, demands every surviving quota be filled exactly, so Remark~\ref{sec:trigger} applies verbatim. The following is brute-force enumeration; its interest lies in what it says about PA-ODR rather than in the algorithm.

\begin{proposition}[Minimum de-reservation for a bounded number of categories]
\label{prop:minderes}
\textsc{MinDeres} is solvable in time $O(\prod_{t\in T}(q^t+1)\cdot\Phi)$, where $\Phi$ is the cost of one capacitated bipartite matching, hence in polynomial time whenever $|T|$ is bounded by a constant.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
A candidate is determined by the numbers $q^t-\hat q^t\in\{0,\dots,q^t\}$, so there are $\prod_t(q^t+1)$ of them and $\hat q^o$ follows. For each, the open assignment, cutoff $e$ and graph $G(e)$ are determined, and realizability is one maximum-cardinality computation. Enumerating and returning a realizable candidate of minimum total de-reservation is correct with the stated running time. Since $q^t\le|I|$ for every category that can ever be filled, there are at most $(|I|+1)^{|T|}$ candidates, polynomial for fixed $|T|$.
\end{proof}

We do not claim this is practical for many categories; whether \textsc{MinDeres} is fixed-parameter tractable in $|T|$, or NP-hard for unbounded $|T|$, is open. What it does establish is that PA-ODR's order dependence is a property of the greedy schedule, not an intrinsic intractability of the objective.

\subsection{Properties of PA-ODR}

Once de-reservation is part of the model, feasibility and non-wastefulness must be judged against the final vector $\hat q$ rather than the initial $q$. By Example~\ref{ex:order} this qualifier is a genuine weakening, not a bookkeeping convention.

\begin{definition}[Non-wastefulness relative to the final effective capacities]
\label{def:nw-hat}
Let $\hat q$ be the final effective capacity vector produced by PA-ODR. A matching $\mu$ is \emph{non-wasteful relative to $\hat q$} if there are no unmatched $i$ and $c\in V_i$ such that adding $i$ to $c$ yields a matching feasible with respect to $\hat q$ and score-feasible.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Termination, running time, feasibility, score-feasibility, non-wastefulness]
\label{prop:paodr-basic}
PA-ODR terminates; after replacing each $q^c$ by $\min\{q^c,|I|\}$ it runs in time polynomial in the input size. Its output is feasible with respect to the final effective capacities, score-feasible, and non-wasteful relative to them.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Termination.} A non-terminating iteration sets $\hat q^{t^\ast}\gets\hat q^{t^\ast}-1$ and $\hat q^o\gets\hat q^o+1$, so the nonnegative integer $\sum_{t}\hat q^t$ falls by one each time and the loop stops after at most $\sum_t q^t$ iterations.

\emph{Running time.} That bound is exponential in the input size if capacities are binary-encoded, so normalize $q^c\gets\min\{q^c,|I|\}$ first. This changes no output, since no category can hold more than $|I|$ students and neither the open assignment nor the capacitated matching is affected by capacity above $|I|$; it is also the normalization under which no seat is de-reserved that no student could have filled. Afterwards $\sum_t\hat q^t\le|I||T|$, so there are $O(|I||T|)$ iterations, each sorting by raw score, building $G(e)$ and solving one \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}, polynomial by Remark~\ref{rem:computation}.

\emph{Feasibility.} In every iteration $o$ receives the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ students, reserve assignment runs on the students not in $\mu_o$ with capacities $\hat q^t$, and edges of $G(e)$ join $i$ only to $t\in\T(i)$. So no student is assigned twice and no category is over capacity; the output is the final iteration's assignment.

\emph{Score-feasibility.} Let $e$ be the final cutoff, so $e_o(\mu)=e$. Every reserve assignee is chosen along an edge of $G(e)$, hence satisfies $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$, so for nonempty $t$ we get $e_t^+(\mu)\ge e-\delta=e_o(\mu)-\delta$, i.e.\ $e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le\delta$; empty categories are vacuous by Definition~\ref{def:scorefeas}.

\emph{Non-wastefulness.} $\mu_o$ holds the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ students, so either everyone is matched or $|\mu_o|=\hat q^o$ and nobody can be added to $o$. At termination $|\mu_t|=\hat q^t$ for every $t$, since otherwise another de-reservation would have occurred. So no unmatched student can be added anywhere within $\hat q$, and a fortiori none yields a matching both feasible for $\hat q$ and score-feasible.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Within-category fairness]
\label{prop:paodr-fair}
The output of PA-ODR is fair within categories in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:fair}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix the final iteration, with cutoff $e$ and output $\mu$, and suppose for contradiction that $t\in T$ and $i,j$ satisfy $t\in\T(i)\cap\T(j)$, $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$, $j\in\mu_t$ and $i\notin M(\mu)$. First, $(i,t)$ is an edge of $G(e)$: as $i\notin M(\mu)$ we have $i\notin\mu_o$, so $i$ is on the left side; as $j\in\mu_t$, the pair $(j,t)$ is an edge, so $\sigma(j)+k\ge e-\delta$, and $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$ gives $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$, while $t\in\T(i)$ holds by assumption. This is the crucial step: a student outranking a matched student inside a category is automatically admissible at the prevailing cutoff, so the cutoff never shields a matched student from a stronger claimant. Now replace $j$ by $i$ in $\mu_t$. The result is a capacitated matching on $G(e)$ of the same cardinality, and its multiset of matched elevated scores replaces $\sigma_t(j)$ by the strictly larger $\sigma_t(i)$, so its sorted score vector is lexicographically strictly larger---contradicting criteria 1 and 2 of \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}.
\end{proof}

Note that the qualifier ``with respect to the final capacity vector'' attaches to Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-basic} but not to this one: PA-ODR satisfies the same, unweakened, within-category fairness as SPSE, and no de-reservation-adjusted version is needed. The reason is Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf} together with the first step above---an upward within-category claim can neither break score-feasibility nor be blocked by the cutoff. Unlike SPSE, though, the property comes not from rejection monotonicity but from optimality of the chosen matching inside the final feasibility graph.

\subsection{What PA-ODR Gives Up}
\label{sec:paodr-limits}

Enforcing score-feasibility is not free, and the price falls on the incentive and envy side. Both failures share a source: PA-ODR fills the open category by raw score without consulting preferences, letting preferences matter only as a tie-breaker.

\begin{example}[PA-ODR admits justified envy]
\label{ex:paodr-envy}
Let $T=\{t_1\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, with $i_1(100,\{t_1\})$ and $i_2(90,\{t_1\})$, both ranking $t_1$ above $o$. PA-ODR gives $o$ the top raw score, so $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $e=100$; then $90+5=95\ge100-10$, so $(i_2,t_1)$ is an edge, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, every capacity is met, and the algorithm stops without de-reserving. The outcome is score-feasible, as $e_o-e_{t_1}^+=100-95=5\le\delta$. Yet $i_1$ sits in $o$ while strictly preferring $t_1$, whose occupant satisfies $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=95<105=\sigma_{t_1}(i_1)$, so $i_1$ has justified envy and Definition~\ref{def:nje} fails.

The score rule does not excuse this failure, which matters because Definition~\ref{def:nje} was left unweakened. Honouring the claim gives $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_o=\{i_2\}$, with $e_o=90$ and $e_{t_1}^+=105$, a gap of $-15\le\delta$: the alternative is score-feasible, so the envy survives the score-relative reading discussed in Section~\ref{sec:model}. It is also exactly what SPSE returns here, both students applying to $t_1$, which retains $i_1$ and rejects $i_2$, who is then held by $o$. The mechanisms differ purely in whose claim they honour, with no feasibility excuse for either.
\end{example}

\begin{proposition}[PA-ODR is not strategy-proof]
\label{prop:paodr-notsp}
There is an instance in which a student obtains a strictly $\succ_i$-better assignment under PA-ODR by misreporting her category preference.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, with $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(94,\{t_1,t_2\})$ and true preferences $t_1\succ_{i_2}t_2\succ_{i_2}o$, $o\succ_{i_3}t_1\succ_{i_3}t_2$. In every run $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$ and $e=100$, and both $i_2$ and $i_3$ clear the bar ($100\ge90$, $99\ge90$), so $G(100)$ has all four edges between $\{i_2,i_3\}$ and $\{t_1,t_2\}$, maximum cardinality is $2$, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:matroid} both are matched. Criterion 2 is silent and criterion 3 decides between $\alpha:i_2\mapsto t_1,\ i_3\mapsto t_2$ and $\beta:i_2\mapsto t_2,\ i_3\mapsto t_1$.

Truthfully, $r_{i_2}(t_1)=1$, $r_{i_2}(t_2)=2$, $r_{i_3}(t_1)=2$, $r_{i_3}(t_2)=3$, so the sorted rank vectors are $(3,1)$ for $\alpha$ and $(2,2)$ for $\beta$; as $(2,2)$ is lexicographically smaller, criterion 3 picks $\beta$ and $i_2$ gets $t_2$. Now let $i_2$ report $t_1\succ o\succ t_2$, demoting $t_2$ below $o$ while keeping $t_1$ on top. Then $\hat r_{i_2}(t_1)=1$, $\hat r_{i_2}(t_2)=3$, so the vectors are $(3,1)$ for $\alpha$ and $(3,2)$ for $\beta$; they agree in the first coordinate and $1<2$ in the second, so criterion 3 picks $\alpha$ and $i_2$ gets $t_1$. Since truly $t_1\succ_{i_2}t_2$, the misreport makes her strictly better off.
\end{proof}

This is the manipulation any egalitarian tie-breaker invites: reporting a category as worse than it is makes every assignment giving it to her look worse for her, which under a min--max rule pays whenever she is the one otherwise sacrificed. It is not an artifact of criterion 3; it reflects that PA-ODR is not a deferred-acceptance mechanism, and by Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} it cannot simply be replaced by one holding capacities fixed.

