\section{Conclusion}

Overlapping reserve categories create a genuine allocation problem because admitted students are counted in exactly one category. Score constraints make feasibility depend on endogenous cutoffs, so non-wastefulness must be read relative to the score rule, and full capacity utilization can fail to exist even when every quota is individually honourable. Within-category fairness survives unchanged, by Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf}; no justified envy sits between the two, in that a score-relative weakening of it would have content, though no result here turns on which reading is taken.

