SPSE keeps the initial capacities and inherits the full incentive and no-envy properties of deferred acceptance but need not be score-feasible. PA-ODR enforces the score constraint by filling the open category first and converting unfulfillable reserve capacity into open capacity, retaining unweakened within-category fairness but losing strategy-proofness and envy-freeness, and retiring reserve seats where an idle seat would have sufficed. Table~\ref{tab:comparison} summarizes. The design tension is thus between fixed reserve capacities and score-feasibility: by Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} score-feasibility cannot be bought merely by choosing a better mechanism with capacities held fixed.

We should not overstate what overlap explains. That a score-elevated mechanism can breach a cutoff-gap rule, and that a de-reservation schedule can be order-dependent, are both visible without it. The specifically overlapping content is narrower and sharper: the routing of a multiply eligible student is an allocation decision that can by itself decide score-feasibility, and the joint-versus-individual gap of Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} is a Hall-type obstruction (Remark~\ref{rem:hall}) vanishing without overlap.

